Subject: [PATCH 1/2] =?UTF-8?q?aarch64:=20peephole=20pass=208=20=E2=80=94?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20hoist=20loop-invariant=20rematerialisations?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The RA rematerialises pure values (big movz/movk constants, adrp+add
address pairs, spilled frame addresses via sp-slot loads) at each use;
inside a loop that is once per trip. This pass hoists a pure def-sequence
above the loop top when the destination register is self-contained in the
loop: exactly one definition (the candidate), no mention before it, only
use-first readers after it, and no mention after the backedge (paths that
skip the original block must not observe a changed value).

Entry edges into the loop top are the fallthrough and every branch from
above the top — each receives a copy of the sequence (entry branches that
read dest are rejected). Branches from inside the loop region to the top
are backedges and need no copy: nothing in the loop defines dest after
the transform, so the value persists across them. Call-free loops only
(a caller-saved dest would die at the call the original per-trip def
survived). sp-slot loads additionally require no store overlapping
[off-7, off+7] anywhere in the loop.

DECISIONS.md [history/session41] blesses this transform for the x86-64
PIE leaq shape; this is the AArch64 twin. Measured: 4+ regression-corpus
shapes lose a per-iteration load (arm_gep_regbase_acc_cache,
gep_family_stride_cse, range_check_fold, vectorize_iv_dependent_base);
size-neutral; the benchmark corpus's latch remats keep a different-value
co-def and are correctly rejected — closing those needs the RA to assign
dedicated registers to loop-invariant remat candidates (RA-06, P0).

Gates: cargo test --lib 1293/0 under -D warnings (+4 fixtures, three of
them negative-soundness: in-loop overlapping store, post-loop reader,
call inside the loop); full ARM differential corpus 379/95 with the fail
set byte-identical to the 820a1077 baseline.
